# Line endings are LF, in the repository and in the working copy, on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true system wide, which would rewrite this tree to
# CRLF on the next checkout. That breaks the cross-platform build in a way that is tedious
# to diagnose: a shell script with CRLF endings fails to execute on the macOS and Linux CI
# runners, reporting a "bad interpreter" error that names a line ending nobody can see.
#
# Everything here is already LF, so this pins the current state rather than changing it.
# Visual Studio, VS Code and the .NET toolchain are all comfortable with LF on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF wherever they came from, or the runner cannot start them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts, kept LF for consistency. PowerShell reads either.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never touched: rewriting a byte inside these corrupts them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.dmg binary
*.zip binary
*.snk binary
